From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller: reconcile the bss table instead of only growing it

mapc_sync_bss() is an upsert and nothing ever deletes, so the bss table becomes
the union of every BSSID a radio has ever had.

That would be harmless if BSSIDs were stable. They are not. Without
`option macaddr`, /usr/share/ucode/wifi/iface.uc derives each BSS address from
a counter incremented in interface preparation order, so a reboot can renumber
them. Measured on bpi-8g across a single restart:

	21. 7.   wlan2 = ...92:7b   wlan2-1 = ...91:79   wlan2-2 = ...91:78
	22. 7.   wlan2 = ...91:7b   wlan2-1 = ...92:79   wlan2-2 = ...92:78

The previous three stay in the table for good. The controller is not even
unaware of it - it correctly marked two of those rows enabled=0 - it simply had
no way to remove them. Rows for a BSS that is never mentioned again keep
whatever enabled value they last had, so the same table holds stale rows in two
different states and nothing distinguishes them from live ones by construction.

Measured across the lab on 2026-07-22: 26 rows for 21 real BSSes, with 6 rows
describing BSSes that were not on the air.

This is the discipline sync_operating_class_rows() already states one screen
above - "recovery wants the current picture, not history" - and the same
mark-and-sweep that patch 961 gave radios. bss was the last table of that
family without one.

The sweep runs once per radio, right after that radio's BSSes have been
upserted from a live Topology Response, and deletes rows for that ruid whose
BSSID is not in the reported set. Nothing references bss(bssid), so this is
free of foreign-key fallout.

It deliberately does nothing when the radio reports no BSS at all, and nothing
when the set is larger than the NOT IN list it is willing to build. An empty
report is far more often a half-parsed CMDU than a radio that genuinely lost
every BSS, and deleting a known set on the strength of nothing is how a
reconcile turns into data loss.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
--- a/src/db/mapc_sync.h
+++ b/src/db/mapc_sync.h
@@ -35,6 +35,11 @@
 /* Upsert the 'bss' row for @iface (parent radio must already be synced). */
 void mapc_sync_bss(struct controller *c, struct netif_iface *iface);
 
+/* Delete 'bss' rows of @r that the agent no longer reports (mark-and-sweep
+ * counterpart to the upsert above; call once per radio after its BSSes are
+ * synced). No-op when the radio reports no BSS at all. */
+void mapc_sync_bss_sweep(struct controller *c, struct netif_radio *r);
+
 /* Rebuild the 'topology_link' edge set from c->topology.dev_list, one txn. */
 void mapc_sync_topology(struct controller *c);
 
--- a/src/db/mapc_sync.c
+++ b/src/db/mapc_sync.c
@@ -414,6 +414,75 @@
 	stmt_finish(c->db, st);
 }
 
+/* Largest BSS set we will build a NOT IN list for. A radio carries a
+ * handful; anything beyond this is not a radio we understand, and the safe
+ * response to not understanding is to delete nothing. */
+#define MAPC_SWEEP_MAX_BSS	16
+
+/* Delete the bss rows of @r that the agent no longer reports.
+ *
+ * mapc_sync_bss() only ever upserts, so the table grows into the union of
+ * every BSSID the radio has ever had. That matters because BSSIDs move:
+ * without `option macaddr` the address is derived from a counter incremented
+ * in interface preparation order (/usr/share/ucode/wifi/iface.uc), so a
+ * reboot can renumber them. Measured on bpi-8g, wlan2/wlan2-1/wlan2-2 swapped
+ * their 91/92 prefixes across a single restart and the previous three stayed
+ * in the table for good; the controller even marked two of them enabled=0 and
+ * still had no way to remove them.
+ *
+ * This is the discipline sync_operating_class_rows() already states -
+ * "recovery wants the current picture, not history" - and the mark-and-sweep
+ * patch 961 gave radios. bss was the last table of that family without one.
+ *
+ * It deliberately does nothing when the radio reports no BSS at all. An empty
+ * report is far more often a half-parsed CMDU than a radio that genuinely
+ * lost every BSS, and deleting a known set on the strength of nothing is how
+ * a reconcile turns into data loss.
+ */
+void mapc_sync_bss_sweep(struct controller *c, struct netif_radio *r)
+{
+	char bssid[MAPC_SWEEP_MAX_BSS][18];
+	struct netif_iface *iface;
+	char sql[160 + 3 * MAPC_SWEEP_MAX_BSS];
+	mapdb_stmt *st = NULL;
+	char ruid[18];
+	int n = 0, i;
+
+	if (!c->db || !r || !r->radio_el)
+		return;
+
+	list_for_each_entry(iface, &r->iflist, list) {
+		if (!iface->bss)
+			continue;
+		if (n >= MAPC_SWEEP_MAX_BSS)
+			return;		/* more than we can express - delete nothing */
+		mac2str(iface->bss->bssid, bssid[n]);
+		n++;
+	}
+
+	if (n == 0)
+		return;
+
+	mac2str(r->radio_el->macaddr, ruid);
+
+	snprintf(sql, sizeof(sql),
+		 "DELETE FROM bss WHERE ruid=? AND bssid NOT IN (");
+	for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
+		strcat(sql, i ? ",?" : "?");
+	strcat(sql, ")");
+
+	if (mapdb_prepare(c->db, sql, &st) != MAPDB_OK) {
+		warn("mapc_sync: bss sweep prepare: %s\n", mapdb_last_error(c->db));
+		return;
+	}
+
+	mapdb_bind_text(st, 1, ruid);
+	for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
+		mapdb_bind_text(st, i + 2, bssid[i]);
+
+	stmt_finish(c->db, st);
+}
+
 /* ---- topology_link ---------------------------------------------------- */
 
 void mapc_sync_topology(struct controller *c)
--- a/src/cntlr_map.c
+++ b/src/cntlr_map.c
@@ -4734,6 +4734,9 @@
 				mapc_sync_radio(c, n, r);
 				list_for_each_entry(bss, &r->iflist, list)
 					mapc_sync_bss(c, bss);
+				/* PATCH 962: drop rows this radio no longer
+				 * reports; the upsert above cannot. */
+				mapc_sync_bss_sweep(c, r);
 			}
 #if (EASYMESH_VERSION >= 6)
 			/* AP-MLD Config TLV (0xE0) rides Topology Response too;
